Existential Suffering
Palliative & End of Life
Profound feeling of meaninglessness, Fear of absolute oblivion, Loss of personal identity/dignity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-029
Reason for Visit: Acute psychological crisis characterized by unmanageable distress, complete refusal of care, and severe agitation driven by profound dread.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient was experiencing an acute, severe panic episode entirely driven by profound psychological distress and dread. The patient was continuously crying, explicitly expressing complete meaninglessness, and refusing all physical care, rendering the spouse completely unable to cope. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to differentiate this acute existential crisis from organic terminal delirium or clinical depression, and to provide immediate psychological first aid and pharmacological anxiolysis to restore safety.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I am nothing. My entire life was a waste, and now I am just waiting to be erased completely. I can't bear this terror."
History of Present Illness: Over the last week, the patient has exhibited profound, explicit questioning of the ultimate meaning, distinct value, or ultimate purpose of their entire life. Tonight, this escalated into severe, distinctly explicitly expressed feelings of complete meaninglessness and an absolute, profound void. The patient vocalizes an explicit, profound fear of complete annihilation, distinct oblivion, and absolute non-existence after death. There are distinctly expressed, profound feelings of complete loss of personal identity and lost individual dignity due to their physical dependence. The spouse reports the patient has severe, explicitly expressed deep regret and profound distinct remorse concerning major past life decisions. The patient expresses an explicit desire specifically for hastened death, strictly driven by an absolute perceived lack of life value, rather than physical pain. There are distinct feelings of profound, absolute explicit isolation and deep disconnectedness from others. This severe, completely intractable explicit psychological distress has been entirely unresponsive to standard pharmacological interventions thus far. The patient explicitly expresses a profound feeling of being a complete burden.
Functional Status: Ambulatory, but pacing continuously in extreme distress.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of self-harm driven by unbearable psychic pain; profound caregiver trauma.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 98% on room air | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient is pacing, wringing their hands, intensely tearful, and making poor eye contact, overwhelmed by psychological agony.
Physical Exam: Physical exam reveals no acute physiological deterioration, no signs of hypoxemia, and no focal neurological deficits. A deep, explicitly detailed psychological clinical interview directly identifies profound meaninglessness explicitly and completely distinct from clinical depression (as there is no true anhedonia to prior memories, only regret) or physical pain. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the observed profound feeling of meaninglessness (vocalized void), the explicit fear of absolute oblivion, and the distinct loss of personal identity/dignity.
Environmental Observations: The room is dimly lit, reflecting the patient's somber, isolating mood.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has taken baseline opioids for physical pain, which have absolutely no effect on this profound psychological agony.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Existential Suffering - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Psychic Crisis)
Differentials:

Hyperactive terminal delirium - Ruled out/unlikely due to the patient's completely intact orientation, logical coherence, and absence of visual hallucinations; the distress is purely cognitive and philosophical.

Major Depressive Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary acute driver, as the distress is explicitly rooted in the fear of non-existence and loss of legacy, rather than simple pervasive anhedonia or chemical slowing.

Uncontrolled physical pain - Ruled out/unlikely as the patient explicitly denies somatic pain, focusing entirely on the "pain in my soul."
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Critical

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in the midst of a severe, acute existential crisis, experiencing unimaginable psychological agony related to the loss of self, legacy, and the fear of oblivion. This "total pain" is manifesting as profound autonomic arousal (tachycardia, pacing) and complete refusal of physical care. Because standard analgesics are entirely ineffective for psychic pain and the patient is expressing a desire for hastened death to escape the dread, delaying assessment until standard hours would subject the patient to torturous psychological suffering and leave the spouse entirely unsupported in a critical psychiatric situation. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely necessary to provide acute empathetic presence, de-escalate the panic with rapid-acting anxiolytics, and establish a secure safety plan.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted a thorough suicide risk assessment. Provided intensive, active listening and validation of the profound existential dread without attempting to offer empty platitudes. Administered stat sublingual lorazepam to blunt the acute, overwhelming autonomic panic and allow the patient to rest tonight.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the overwhelming dread of the "void" closing in (trigger), deliberately pick up the photo album on the nightstand and describe one specific, positive memory out loud to your spouse (behavior). This actively grounds you in the undeniable reality of your lived history, temporarily breaking the cycle of perceived meaninglessness (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent, high-priority referral to the palliative care pastoral/spiritual care worker and clinical psychologist for assessment tomorrow morning.
DME/Safety (Optional): Immediate removal of all lethal means (medications, sharps) from the immediate environment.
Follow-up: Multidisciplinary palliative team review tomorrow; GP review in 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient makes an active attempt to self-harm, attempts to leave the house in a state of severe agitation, or if the spouse feels unsafe or unable to manage the patient's distress, they must call 000 immediately for police and ambulance support.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Medications can only take the edge off the physical panic associated with these thoughts; they cannot cure the profound philosophical questions you are facing.
Clinical Course: Existential distress requires intensive, ongoing therapeutic dialogue and spiritual support to find a framework of peace before death.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Spouse: Do not leave the patient alone tonight. Secure all medications. Do not try to argue with their despair; simply sit with them and validate that their fear is heard and understood.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: (To Patient) The sheer terror you are feeling is your mind grappling with the heaviest burden a human can face. The medication I gave you will not erase the thoughts, but it will physically slow your racing heart and calm your nerves enough so that you can simply rest for the next few hours.
Actionable Steps: Agree to a safety contract for tonight. Allow your spouse to sit with you, even in silence. Accept that these terrifying thoughts are a normal, though painful, part of this journey.

